2026-09-29T02:55:00-07:00 RESULT DQS JOIN FIX DOES NOT MOVE THE MEASUREMENT. Full calibrated tb_top at 5e81104 (DDR3_DQS released = z, controller owns the pin) produces MEAS lines BIT-IDENTICAL to the pre-fix run: MEAS0 002eb0ba / MEAS1 001a4fee / MEAS2 00224fef / MEAS3 002e4ff0 / MEAS4 xxxxxxxx, TOP_RC=0, decoder rc=1 "missing snapshot(s) 4 of 0..4". The join fix is still correct and worth keeping (it makes rburst observable: 0 -> 58), but it changes nothing end to end, which is the expected consequence of gowin_prim_models.v driving DQSR90 = FCLK unconditionally and IDES8_MEM having no READ/HOLD input: dqs_read cannot influence what any bench captures. Evidence: evidence/sim-final-dqs.log.
2026-09-29T02:55:01-07:00 BLOCKED UNCHANGED. (1) MEAS4 is x, so meas_snap(2'd4) never ran -- the phase still leaves via the data-mismatch path. (2) MEAS0..3 remain non-monotonic, with MEAS0 holding the end-of-run pclk (002eb0ba) though s0 is captured at READ_DONE; the snapshot registers are written at the wrong point or overwritten. A reset on s1_*/s4_* would make MEAS4 print zeros and let the decoder emit a rate for a phase that never ran -- that is a fabricated measurement and is deliberately NOT done. 400 MB/s remains unmeasured and unrefuted.
2026-09-29T03:05:00-07:00 FINDING MEAS0..3 SLOTS ARE VISITED OUT OF ORDER. Sorted by pclk value the emitted snapshots read s1 (0x001a4fee) < s2 (0x00224fef) < s3 (0x002e4ff0) < s0 (0x002eb0ba). s0 has exactly ONE writer -- meas_snap(2'd0) at READ_DONE, ddr3_top.v:421 -- and m_pclk is monotonic, so a value captured at READ_DONE cannot exceed one captured at the end of WIPE, which runs after it. The only consistent reading is that the state machine RE-ENTERS READ_DONE after the bulk phases, so s0 is overwritten late with a near-end counter value while s1..s3 keep their earlier values. Consequence: the MEAS0-3 deltas are not a sequence of phase deltas at all, so even the WRITE_BLOCK / VERIFY_BLOCK rates those deltas imply are not trustworthy. The re-entry path is NOT yet traced in the RTL -- this is inferred from the emitted values, and the next step is to find which transition returns to READ_DONE.
2026-09-29T03:05:01-07:00 NOTE   READ_BURST now has TWO meas_snap(2'd4) writers: ddr3_top.v:539 (completion path) and :577 (data-mismatch path, added so a failing run still reports its counters). Not a bug -- both mean "end of READ_BURST" -- but it means a mismatching run CAN emit a MEAS4, and a MEAS4 from an error path must never be read as a rate. The distinction has to survive into whatever consumes it.

2026-09-29T03:34:41-07:00 RESULT  MEAS4 ROOT CAUSE FOUND AND FIXED -- it was a 2-BIT SNAPSHOT PORT, not a missing snapshot and not a mismatch abort. `task meas_snap; input [1:0] ph;` cannot hold 4. Both READ_BURST call sites passed `2'd4`, which truncates to `2'b00`, so BOTH wrote SLOT 0 and slot 4 had NO REACHABLE WRITER anywhere in the design. The `2'd4:` case label inside the task was itself unreachable. Icarus printed "warning: Numeric constant truncated to 2 bits" three times per compile, at exactly the two call sites and the one case label, in every build log since the task was written.
2026-09-29T03:34:41-07:00 TRACE  Identified by instrumentation, not by reading the MEAS text, as instructed. One -DTB_TRACE run, $display in each of the three FINISH arms plus one inside meas_snap printing the resolved slot and the m_* sources. Pre-fix, the clean-completion path DID run and meas_snap DID execute -- with slot=0:
                    TB-TRACE EXIT-A READ_BURST clean-completion pclk=3059898 wr=131075 rd=65539 issued=8192 recvd=8191
                    TB-TRACE MEAS-SNAP slot=0 m_pclk=3059898 wr=131075 rd=65539 m_rf=1
                  So the 02:35 claim on this line -- "meas_snap(2'd4) never executed: the phase left via the data-mismatch path" -- is WRONG on both counts. It executed, and it left via the CLEAN completion path (issued=8192, recvd=8191), not a mismatch. Item 2 of the review is answered too: m_pclk/m_wr/m_rd were NOT x at the call, so the source was never the problem.
2026-09-29T03:34:41-07:00 RESULT  The MEAS0 NON-MONOTONICITY IS THE SAME BUG, not a separate one. The trace shows slot 0 has TWO writers: READ_DONE wrote it at m_pclk=1200101, then the READ_BURST completion overwrote it at m_pclk=3059898 (last write wins). That is exactly the reported symptom -- MEAS0 holding the END-of-run pclk and exceeding MEAS1..MEAS3 while slots 1,2,3 are monotonic among themselves. Nothing re-entered any phase. The "late write" observation was right; the slot it was aimed at was the defect. One fix closes both long-standing puzzles, so the separate MEAS0 investigation is closed rather than left unexplained.
2026-09-29T03:34:41-07:00 FIX      src/ddr3_top.v: meas_snap port widened to 3 bits (MEAS_SLOT_W=3), call sites and case labels use 3'd0..3'd4, plus an elaboration-time $error guard on $bits(ph) so a re-narrowed port or a future slot 5+ fails loudly instead of silently retargeting a snapshot. The post-fix compile emits ZERO truncation warnings. Confirming run: five DISTINCT slots, monotonic -- slot0 1200101, slot1 1724398, slot2 2248687, slot3 3035120, slot4 3059898.
2026-09-29T03:34:41-07:00 CORRECTION  On the review's item 4. There is NO third exit from READ_BURST. `grep 'state <= FINISH'` returns exactly 3 lines and `state` has exactly one driver, but line 612 is in VERIFY_BLOCK, not READ_BURST -- a DIFFERENT phase that runs BEFORE it. Both READ_BURST exits do snapshot. And the VERIFY_BLOCK arm is NOT dead code: fast_mode is (state == READ_BURST), so fast_mode is 0 throughout VERIFY_BLOCK, the legacy FSM owns the datapath, and `data_ready` -- driven only from the legacy {READ, cycle 7} arm -- is exactly the signal that phase is built around. Deleting it would have removed the only legacy read-data check in the design on a phase attribution off by one case label. LEFT IN PLACE and commented with this reasoning at the call site. Its lack of a snapshot is argued correct: it is a MID-PHASE abort, not a phase end, so snapshotting slot 3 there would write a half-phase value into the slot the decoder differences against WIPE/WRITE_BLOCK.
2026-09-29T03:34:41-07:00 NO RATE CLAIMED. This was a targeted confirming run of a slot-write bug, not a cadence measurement. The 503.18 figure is untouched and was not re-run for. Unchanged and still open: the read capture remains unexercised at the design point (bench drives DDR3_DQS; IDES8_MEM has no READ/HOLD input). Evidence: evidence/meas4_slot_width_root_cause.md, evidence/sim-exitprobe-run.log, evidence/sim-meas4slot3-run.log.

2026-09-29T04:12:00-07:00 RESULT  TWO MORE DEFECTS ON THE SAME PATH, both found by instrumenting the WIRE rather than by reading the MEAS text. After the 3-bit slot fix the DUT-side dump completed (meas_go=0, print_meas=0, five monotonic slots incl. slot4) and yet ZERO bytes reached the bench receiver: grep -c 'UART|' = 0. That is NOT "MEAS4 still missing" -- the snapshot was there. The data was written and never transported.
2026-09-29T04:12:00-07:00 DEFECT-4  src/print.v send block. `uart_en<=1'b0;` and `if(uart_en && uart_bz) seq_head<=seq_head+1;` and `if(seq_head!=seq_tail && !uart_bz) uart_en<=1'b1;` were three independent nonblocking statements assigning uart_en. On the cycle where a byte completes AND the queue is non-empty, uart_en is scheduled to 0 while seq_head advances, so the transmitter is never handed the next byte and waits forever for a wr_en pulse nothing generates. Fixed by reloading the transmitter in the same cycle the byte completes. Invisible from PRINT-STATE alone: head==tail at the end makes the FIFO look drained.
2026-09-29T04:12:00-07:00 DEFECT-5  The dump driver issued faster than the print FSM accepts. `int_print` takes a request only while print_state==PRINT_IDLE_STATE, and the FSM needs TWO cycles to leave IDLE (IDLE->WAIT on the spin_state toggle, then WAIT->WORK); a second call in that window is DROPPED SILENTLY -- the macro has no return value and no error path. The pre-fix gate (print_meas==print_meas_p) permits consecutive idle cycles, so roughly every other label was lost. Fixed with a `meas_req` one-label-in-flight flag. Same failure mode as the original MEAS4 print-machine gap: a run that still prints ENDMEAS and looks well formed while silently dropping data.
2026-09-29T04:12:00-07:00 DEFECT-6 (ROOT CAUSE OF THE ZERO BYTES)  `localparam FREQ=99_800_000` is a NOMINAL figure, but the real pclk is 99.5625 MHz. FREQ feeds tx.clk_freq, so TX_CLK_MAX = 99800000/115200-1 = 865 pclk per bit and the transmitter emits a bit every 8688.0 ns instead of the bench's assumed 8681 ns. That is 7.0 ns of drift PER BIT: after 10 bits the sample point has slipped 70 ns, 8.7% of a bit, and framing is destroyed at about 12 bytes. MEASURED, not derived: a new wire probe counted negedges_on_wire=26 but tx_start_bytes=11 and the receiver decoded ZERO complete lines, on a 223-byte dump. Fixed: FREQ = 99_562_500. Checked the blast radius -- FREQ has exactly one other consumer, REFRESH_COUNT, which is 773 under BOTH values, so the refresh cadence does not move.
2026-09-29T04:12:00-07:00 NOTE  No run in this project has EVER produced a DUT-printed MEAS line. The 5 `UART|` lines in evidence/sim-read-rate-run.log (which reported TB-COMPLETE) were BENCH-FORGED by the pre-3555fd9 dump block, together with its own `TB-COMPLETE snapshots read at FINISH`. That forgery is what hid defects 4, 5 and 6 for this long, and stopping it is what made them findable. This is the strongest argument yet for the tb receiver: it is the only thing in the design that can tell "the counter finished walking" from "the data was transmitted".
2026-09-29T04:12:00-07:00 NO RATE CLAIMED. Still a targeted bug-fix verification, not a cadence measurement. The 503.18 figure is untouched and was not re-run for. Evidence: evidence/meas4_slot_width_root_cause.md, which carries a full EVIDENCE INDEX table citing every one of the nine run logs committed here and what each one establishes (the four byte-identical 26/11 rows are the disproof).

2026-09-29T05:40:00-07:00 CORRECTION  The 04:12 entry above attributes the zero-wire-bytes to the FREQ/bit-period error and calls print.v's send block a "drop the next byte" stall. BOTH ATTRIBUTIONS ARE WRONG, and the wire probe disproves them: after fixing them the probe read negedges_on_wire=26 tx_start_bytes=11 -- BYTE-IDENTICAL to the pre-fix run. A transport bit-period change cannot leave a byte-count counter bit-identical, so neither was the active cause. Correcting the record rather than leaving a plausible story in the log.
2026-09-29T05:40:00-07:00 REAL-DEFECT-1 (print.v, mine)  My own "fix" to the send block was the bug. Holding uart_en high across the whole ~8640-pclk transmission made the completion test `uart_en && uart_bz` true on EVERY cycle of it, so seq_head advanced once per CLOCK instead of once per BYTE -- the queue drained ~800x too fast. Measured: seq_head reached 54 while the transmitter had started only 11 bytes. seq_head==seq_tail at end of run is exactly what hid this: a 54-byte stream looks perfectly drained. Correct form advances the head once per byte on the FALLING edge of uart_bz (tx_busy = state!=IDLE), re-arming uart_en on that same edge. After this fix the probe read tx_start_bytes=54 and negedges_on_wire=131 -- the FIFO now advances once per byte, confirmed by an independent counter.
2026-09-29T05:40:00-07:00 REAL-DEFECT-2 (ddr3_top.v, pre-existing)  With the FIFO correct, only 54 bytes were ever ENQUEUED for a stream needing 118 (FINISH status block) + 218 (dump) = 336. `int_print` accepts a request only while print_state==PRINT_IDLE_STATE and leaves no trace when it drops one, so a label issued on back-to-back idle cycles is silently thrown away. Fixed with print_stat_q/print_meas_q request-pending flags released BY THE CONSUMER (on print_state leaving IDLE, which proves the spin_state toggle was taken) rather than by a timer. A first attempt that cleared the flag one cycle after setting it lost every label outright -- documented in-code so it is not reintroduced.
2026-09-29T05:40:00-07:00 PARTIAL  FREQ=99_562_500 and the uart_tx_V2 TX_CLK_MAX off-by-one are REAL defects, measured independently: the true ratio is 864.26, the old expression gave 863 pclk = 8667.9 ns/bit = 12.6 ns short, and 864 pclk is correct (8678.0 ns, -2.6 ns, inside the 50% sampling window). An elaboration $error guard now fails on 863 and on 865 and passes only on 864 -- verified by evaluating the guard predicate on all three. These were NOT the cause of the zero bytes and are not claimed to be; they are kept because a 12.6 ns/bit error would independently destroy a 223-byte stream. REFRESH_COUNT is 773 under both the old and new FREQ, so the refresh cadence does not move.
2026-09-29T05:40:00-07:00 STATUS  The 5-slot fix is CONFIRMED by trace at both region sizes (full 64 Ki-word: slot4=3059898, monotonic 1.20M/1.72M/2.25M/3.04M/3.06M; TB_BULK=64: slot4=1201941, monotonic). The remaining work is the two print-path defects above. Still NO RATE CLAIMED and the 503.18 figure is untouched -- every run here is a bug-fix verification, not a cadence measurement.
